From timh at insightful.com  Wed Mar  2 21:12:22 2005
From: timh at insightful.com (Tim Hesterberg)
Date: Wed, 02 Mar 2005 13:12:22 -0800
Subject: Re:
In-Reply-To: <004e01c51ea4$4213c970$ae1ad284@BIO041>
References: <004e01c51ea4$4213c970$ae1ad284@BIO041>
Message-ID: <SE2KEXCH01tbS8oLhWT00001086@se2kexch01.insightful.com>

Bill Shipley wrote:
>Hello.  I want to fit a smoother spline (or an equivalent local
>regression method) to a set of data (y=f(x)+e) in which the value of the
>1st derivative (slope) at the first unique x value is constrained to a
>specific value but can change afterwards specified by the smoother df.  
>
>Is it possible to do this?  If so, how?

One approach is to use regression splines.
For example bs() creates a basis for a regression of the
form
        y ~ bs(x)

Similarly, ns() creates a basis.  Whereas bs() creates a basis
with no restrictions, ns() creates a basis with the restrictions
that the second derivative is zero at the extreme knots.

You need to create a basis with a different restriction,
that the first derivative equal to zero at the first unique x value;
then a regression of the form
        y ~ mybasis(x)
would set the first derivative to zero at the first x.  To get
the derivative you want, use offset, e.g.
        y ~ offset(0.01 * x) + mybasis(x)

ns() uses calls to spline.des and qr.* to create its basis.
You can do the same; it may take some work.

Tim Hesterberg

