From spencer.graves at pdf.com  Thu Mar 10 21:50:31 2005
From: spencer.graves at pdf.com (Spencer Graves)
Date: Thu, 10 Mar 2005 13:50:31 -0800
Subject: Re: orthogonal polynomial matrix for unequal intervals
In-Reply-To: <327C2434A3DA2D4CB61554D644BAE48B614768@LRCPSSMBMXMB001.lrc.ad.gov.on.ca>
References: <327C2434A3DA2D4CB61554D644BAE48B614768@LRCPSSMBMXMB001.lrc.ad.gov.on.ca>
Message-ID: <4230C127.4010507@pdf.com>

     Can you get the SAS numbers to more precision?  Let P4 be your 
matrix of orthogonal polynomials up to order 4: 

     P4 <- poly(c(1,2,3,5,6),4)

     Then crossprod(P4) = t(P4)%*%P4 should be a 4x4 identity matrix.  
To evaluate the numerical precision, sum the absolute values: 

     sum(abs(crossprod(P4)-diag(4)))

     S-Plus 6.2 for Windows gave me 6.7e-15 for this.  R 2.0.1 gave me 
9.4e-16.  I don't know if this almost one additional digit for R is just 
a fluke or is typical of what we would see over a wide variety of 
similar comparisons. 

     I copied the numbers from your email into Excel and then pulled 
them into S-Plus (and R) as SAS.Ortho, with columns 2:5 being the SAS 
matrix and 6:9 being the numbers that S-Plus displayed for you and that 
you reported in your email.  With this, I got the following: 

sum(abs(crossprod(SAS.Ortho[, 2:5]) - diag(4)))
[1] 3.296815e-006
sum(abs(crossprod(SAS.Ortho[, 6:9]) - diag(4)))
[1] 3.278993e-007

     The drop in precision from 6.7e-15 to 3.3e-7 for S-Plus is due to 
rounding.  I don't know if the drop in precision from 3.3e-7 for S-Plus 
to 3.3e-6 for SAS is a fluke or is typical of what we would see in most 
comparisons of this nature.  To check this, it would help to do this 
orthogonality check completely within SAS -- or failing that to export 
more digits if you can. 

     If you get the SAS numbers to more precision, would you please 
inform this list?  Crudely similar questions arise routinely, and it 
would be interesting to have this comparison in the archives -- 
especially if you could do it all in SAS and compare with S-Plus in 
other examples. 

     hope this helps. 
     spencer graves  

