From tjagger at blarg.net  Fri Mar 11 17:39:33 2005
From: tjagger at blarg.net (Thomas Jagger)
Date: Fri, 11 Mar 2005 10:39:33 -0700
Subject: Re: pi.fit option in predict.glm
In-Reply-To: <884331pggp274etkol9earkqck1fn1edhn@4ax.com>
References: <884331pggp274etkol9earkqck1fn1edhn@4ax.com>
Message-ID: <20050311173936.4872CF3A67@mail.blarg.net>

Hello, in the function predict.glm, while it seems the calculation of link
and response confidence limits are correct those for the prediction
intervals are not since the variance component due to the response is just
the dispersion value, which is not correct as far as I know. For example if
the response is Poisson the variance is the mean and not the dispersion
which is one. I would not use the prediction intervals for the response
anyway, if you must assume a "normal" distribution for the prediction
interval then the variance used in calculating the prediction interval might
be  family()$var(fitted value) * dispersion + se(fitted value)^2.

See predict.glm for details, look at the type="response" section.

I use sampling methods to generate prediction intervals, to avoid issues
with non Gaussian distributions when the dispersion is assumed to be one for
single parameter distributions (binomial, Poisson).

1. sample from the type="link" standard error parameters assuming a normal
distribution with mean as the fitted value and standard deviation from the
se=T return value. (These values seem correct from the code).
2. Use the inverse link to generate the mean. In the gamma distribution the
parameters are associated with the mean a/b and variance a/b^2. Note that
a=1/dispersion, but you could just use Gamma()$var(fitted)*dispersion as
well for a/b^2. Do the math to get a,b a=1/dispersion, b=a/mu (thus the term
"rate" parameter. (So var=dispersion*mu^2 as a check). 
2. Sample from the distribution with the associated sampled parameters.
3. Use the 95% "credible" interval, say using 
        quantile(x,c(.025,.975))

If you have over dispersed Poisson or Binomial data, this approach will not
work as well. Here you might consider using a Generalized Linear Mixed
Effects Model. 

V6.1 Splus Code. I noticed this issue generating confidence intervals for
Poisson regression. Since it was for production application, I wanted to
make sure the code does what it says it is supposed to. Statistical analysis
is not a black box application, and while we would like to think that all
code runs and does what it is supposed to do it may not. 

Tom

