Message-ID: <031420052259.20076.4236176A0000CC6200004E6C22058861729C0B9D0E99050C0E0D9C@comcast.net>

Hi,

 

Re: setValidity under S-PLUS 6.2 on AIX and Windows.

 

After many years of putting it off, I finally am getting seriously into SV4 classes and methods, for a new project.  So far I must confess to being not unhappy with them, but it has not been an entirely smooth trip.  My first stumble was over class "named", which wisely seems not to have infected R.  (Why does list(a = 1) have class "list", but after x <- list(a = 1); names(x) <- "a", x has class "named"?  Or rather, I think I know why in the proximal sense, but I still view it as an annoying bug.) 

 

Now, I have encountered another difficulty, concerning setValidity.  I have corresponded with Insightful support about this.  They have been very helpful and prompt in replying.  They tell me that I have "encountered a bug in the implementation of validity checking. The bug has been here since S-PLUS 5.0 and you are the the first person to report it." 

Details about the bug are given at the end of this email.  In short, there seem to be two problems:  (1) the validity function is called way too often; and (2) the validity function never does anything useful, unless called explicitly via validObject().  As far as I can see, by using setValidity you get all the cost and more, but very little gain.  Until this is fixed, I intend never to set validity functions as part of class definitions, and write my own validObj function to mimic what validObject would do for me if I DID set a validity function.

I have seen a little discussion about SV4 object validation in the R mailing list archives (Jan 2004), but found nothing directly relevant in the s-news archives.  I am surprised that such a bug could go for at least 7 years without being reported.  Are people STILL not using SV4 classes and methods in S-PLUS?  Or do those (few?) that do use them not concern themselves with object validity checking?  Anyway, I thought there might be one, or possibly two, others out there who might find it useful to know about this issue.

 

Cheers,

Scott

 

Scott Chasalow

Bristol-Myers Squibb

Statistical Genetics and Biomarkers

 

### 
# Testing validity methods. 
### 

setClass("junk2", representation(x = "numeric"), prototype = list( 
x = numeric(0))) 

setValidity("junk2", function(object) { 
out <- if (length(object@x) == 0 || object@x > 0) TRUE else 
"Slot x must be positive" 
cat("\nFrom validity method:\n") 
print(out) 
out 
}) 

### 
# Why is the validity method being called here? I'm not doing a 
# permanent assignment! And this is doubly wasteful here, since 
# (if I remove the cat() and print() statements that are here just 
# for debugging), the validity function call fails to report its 
# return value, generate any kind of warning or error, or give the 
# slightest clue that it ever was called. 
### 
new("junk2", x = -2) 
# An object of class "junk2" 
# 
# Slot "x": 
# [1] -2 
# 
# 
# From validity method: 
# [1] "Slot x must be positive" 

### 
# Now, do a permanent as
 signment. Here, I DID expect the validity 
# method to be called, as it is. But still, it does nothing remotely 
# useful. I pay the price of calling the validity method, but create 
# an invalid junk2 object with narry a peep from SPLUS. 
### 
junk3 <- new("junk2", x = -2) 
# 
# From validity method: 
# [1] "Slot x must be positive" 

### 
# Same problem with a temporary assignment inside a function call: 
# validity method should NOT be called here. And if it IS to be 
# called, it could at least do something useful. 
### 
(function() {x <- new("junk2"); x })() 
# An object of class "junk2" 
# 
# Slot "x": 
# numeric(0) 
# 
# 
# From validity method: 
# [1] T 

### 
# Now finally, if I call validObject() explicitly, the validity method 
# does something useful. 
### 
validObject(junk3) 
# 
# From validity method: 
# [1] "Slot x must be positive" 
# Problem in va
 lidObject(junk3): invalid object: : Slot x must be positive 
# 
# Use traceback() to see the call stack 

From dsmith at insightful.com  Tue Mar 15 16:51:36 2005
From: dsmith at insightful.com (David Smith)
Date: Tue, 15 Mar 2005 08:51:36 -0800
Subject: Re: setValidity
Message-ID: <EDAC416B87ECCA44BEAB4D0CF48034EF2C691C@se2kexch01.insightful.com>

Scott has stumbled upon one of the differences between S and S-PLUS.
Validity checking was introduced as part of S version 4 in the original
implementation by John Chambers, which was described in the Green Book
(Programming With Data).  When Insightful created S-PLUS based on Sv4, not
all attributes of Sv4 were incorporated into the S language included with
S-PLUS, and the validity checking feature was one of them. Validity checking
(and the function setValidity) aren't documented in S-PLUS for that reason.
(That's the likely reason why no-one has reported problems with it before.) 

Since the introduction of Sv4 more than five years ago, there have been a
number of divergences between S as described in the Green Book and S as
implemented in S-PLUS.  As a result, you should refer to the S-PLUS
documentation (the Programmer's Guide) for current information S in S-PLUS,
not the Green Book.

# David Smith

