Message-ID: <031520051912.18212.4237338B000651740000472422007348309C0B9D0E99050C0E0D9C@comcast.net>

Thanks for that explanation David.  While I appreciate the general point, I fear it is in this particular case not entirely accurate.

 

First, validity checking WAS (and IS) incorporated into S-PLUS.  Incorporation may or may not have been intentional, but in either case it was not done correctly.  If I set a validity function for an S4 class, it gets called frequently, even when it makes little sense to do so.  Now I grant you that, except for the overhead incurred by all these useless function calls, the calls ordinarily are invisible to the user.  But I find that an odd way to disincorporate a feature. If the call is useless, much better to not call the validity function in the first place.

 

Second, validity checking and even function setValidity ARE documented in S-PLUS; just not very well.  Function setValidity is mentioned at the bottom of page 290 of the S-PLUS 6 Programmer's Guide for UNIX/Linux.  (That's probably where I learned about its existence in the first place.)  Although setValidity does not have a real page in the Language Reference, function validObject DOES (although its description appears to me to be incorrect).  And the Language Reference page for function setClass not only describes argument "validity", but uses it in the examples section.

 

This stumbling block feels rather like a bug in the code or documentation or both, not a missing feature.

 

With best regards,

Scott

 

Scott Chasalow

Bristol-Myers Squibb

Statistical Genetics and Biomarkers

