From tplate at blackmesacapital.com  Thu Mar 17 17:49:34 2005
From: tplate at blackmesacapital.com (Tony Plate)
Date: Thu, 17 Mar 2005 10:49:34 -0700
Subject: Re: if vs. ifelse
In-Reply-To: <BAY10-F370A2BDD5DFF701871A3C1D6490@phx.gbl>
References: <BAY10-F370A2BDD5DFF701871A3C1D6490@phx.gbl>
Message-ID: <4239C32E.8040700@blackmesacapital.com>

The first few lines of the documentation for ifelse() reads:

> DESCRIPTION:
> Places values into an object like test according to the logical values in test.
> USAGE:
> ifelse(test, yes, no)

So, ifelse(test, yes, no) returns an object like 'test'.  In your case, 
'test' was a vector with one element.  Since 'yes' was numeric (and 
'test' was TRUE), a numeric vector with one element is returned.  The 
rest of the elements of the arguments 'yes' and 'no' are ignored.

'if' and 'ifelse' are quite different: 'if' is a control structure for 
conditionally executing different blocks of code.  'ifelse' is a 
vectorized function that conditionally selects elements from one or 
another vector.

Does that clear things up?

-- Tony Plate

