Subject: SUMMARY re: Problem in .Fortran subroutine mnb1?
In-Reply-To: <Pine.OSF.4.62.0503140953470.476126@hypatia.unh.edu>
References: <Pine.OSF.4.62.0503140953470.476126@hypatia.unh.edu>
Message-ID: <Pine.OSF.4.62.0503171506350.270076@hypatia.unh.edu>

Thanks go to John Fennick, Patric Burns, and Bill Dunlap for helping me 
figure this out!

The problem (copied below) ended up being that my random subsample would 
occassionally be a set of subjects which all had identical values for one 
of the independent variables.  Trying to fit a model to one such subsample 
would produce the error.

This was confirmed by using the try() command with the polr() model, as 
suggested by Dunlap:

        ans <- try(polr(...))
        if (inherits(ans, "Error")) {
                # polr() had an error. then use sink() to export data
                # for examination
        } else {
                # ans is the output of polr(), treat it
                # as I would a standard polr() output
        }

It worked wonderfully!  Thanks for your help!

Cheers,
Andy

