hmm, excuse me Tim, but I'm not convinced ..... I would use   
     answer <-  if(test) x else y

I have been using more and more (and advocating the use of) 

   Var <-  if(Cond) A else B

also in forms like

  Var[long.winded.index] <-  
       if (very.long.cond) {
            quite.a.few.things
       } else { 
            some.other.stuff
       }

In my opinion,  ifelse() is very much overused by people.
Also the fact that ifelse(Cond, A,B) is often inefficient by
computing both full-length vectors A and B and afterwards
throwing away half the results is not particularly attractive.

OTOH,  S has always been the nice functional language that it
is, and hence also  "if(..) .. else .." is a valid expression
that can be used on the RHS of assignment.

    Tim> #
    Tim> # If more than three arguments are supplied, then y should be
    Tim> # a second test; 
    Tim> #     ifelse1(test1, x, test2, u, v)
    Tim> # is equivalent to
    Tim> #     if(test){x} else if(test2) {y} else {v}
    Tim> # This may be iterated; there should be an odd number of arguments.
    Tim> if(test) x else if(missing(..1))
    Tim> y
    Tim> else ifelse1(y, ...)
    Tim> }

that (idea of recursively going) *is* nice and not covered by
the above.  However I think it can loose on the "readibility" /
"self documentation" side...

Martin Maechler, ETH Zurich

