>>>>>> "Tim" == Tim Hesterberg <timh at insightful.com>
>>>>>>     on 17 Mar 2005 15:30:11 -0800 writes:
>
>    >> I'm having problems with if vs. ifelse:
>    >> ...
>
>    Tim> I think Tony Plate answered your question nicely.
>    Tim> I'll chip in with a function that seems to do what you're looking for:
>
>    Tim> ifelse1 <-
>    Tim> function(test, x, y, ...)
>    Tim> {
>    Tim> # if(test) return x, else return y.
>    Tim> # Like ifelse(), except that test is length 1, and x or y
>    Tim> # is returned as is (whatever its length).
>    Tim> #     ifelse1(test, x, y)
>    Tim> # is equivalent to
>    Tim> #     if(test){x} else {y}.
>    Tim> # This is particularly useful for assignment;
>    Tim> #     answer = ifelse1(test, x, y)
>    Tim> # is equivalent to
>    Tim> #     if(test) answer = x else answer = y
>
>hmm, excuse me Tim, but I'm not convinced ..... I would use   
>     answer <-  if(test) x else y

That is equivalent to using
     answer <-  ifelse1(test, x, y)

ifelse1 has some advantages:
* Better formatting when using emacs ESS.
* Don't have to worry about the "else" being split from the "if"; e.g.
     answer <-  if(test) x
       else y
  This can cause a problem when the code is pasted to the prompt one
  line at a time.
* The parentheses clearly show the encapsulation.
* More consistent with the usual syntax:
     answer <- someFunction(args)

I first created ifelse1 for use in S+SeqTrial, to improve the
readability of the code.

There is also a third alternative
  if(test) answer <- x else answer <- y
which I dislike.  Both of the other solutions begin with
  answer <- ...
which makes clear that the point of the calculations is to assign
something to answer.

>In my opinion,  ifelse() is very much overused by people.
>Also the fact that ifelse(Cond, A,B) is often inefficient by
>computing both full-length vectors A and B and afterwards
>throwing away half the results is not particularly attractive.

That is true with ifelse.  ifelse1() only evaluates one of x or y.
For an example of the speed hit using ifelse() rather than
computing only the relevant values, see
        http://www.insightful.com/Hesterberg/articles/EfficientSplus.txt
and search for "dhyper".

