From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Wed Mar 23 20:13:09 2005
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Wed, 23 Mar 2005 20:13:09 +0000
Subject: Re: SDD file format
In-Reply-To: <003a01c52fe2$d10fdfc0$4a1414ac@prosanos.local>
References: <003a01c52fe2$d10fdfc0$4a1414ac@prosanos.local>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.61.0503232001250.492@gannet.stats>

On Wed, 23 Mar 2005, Robert Kingan wrote:

> Is the .SDD file format documented publicly? I would like to write some
> C++ routines that would produce .SDD files for import into S-PLUS. Is
> this feasible?

By `.SDD' do you mean (one of) the formats produced by data.dump?  It is 
not specifically `.SDD', and indeed that extension I have only seen on 
Windows.

I am unaware of any public documentation.  However, simpler cases 
including the oldStyle=T case have been reverse-engineered from examples 
at least twice.  R's foreign package has code to read such files which 
should help you write them, or just dump the sort of objects you wish to 
import and mimic the dumps you see.

However, I would not do things that way.  The .Call interface allows you 
to create S objects in a fairly-well documented way, and I would write 
files in a suitable binary format (data.dump is ASCII and reading ASCII is 
quite wasteful for numeric data) and write some C code to convert it into 
an S object. Indeed, you may even be able to read binary files directly 
via readRaw or readMapped.

