The list of S function is predominantly going to be support 
functions you'd never call - as Richard says, the S 
Language Reference Window is what you want.  If you want to 
see a list of all functions together with arguments they 
take, have a look at this code:-
toSearch <- search()[-1]
funList <- sapply(toSearch, objects, class="function")
funMat <- sapply(funList, function(i) {
                argList <- sapply(i, function(txt) {
                N <- length(myFun <- args(get(txt)))
                funCall <- paste(paste(names(myFun), 
                        ifelse(as.character(myFun) 
== "", "", "="), 
                        as.character(myFun))[-N], 
collapse=", ")
                c(txt, funCall)
        })
})
funDf <- data.frame(Database=rep(toSearch, sapply(funList, 
length)), Function=unlist(sapply(funMat, function(x) x
[1,])), Args=unlist(sapply(funMat, function(x) x[2,])))
html.table(funDf, "temp.html", htm=T)
systemOpen("temp.html")
This is overkill really, and takes a few minutes, but it 
will get you a list of all functions together with their 
arguments (and defaults).  This is just a quick piece of 
code - there's probably lots of ways of doing this in a 
more efficient way ...
Cheers,
Rich.
PS: Insightful used to do a really good document called 
the "Function Guide" - not sure if you can still get hold 
of this.  I think it is now out of date, but it is still a 
good document â

