From a.wong at home.nl  Fri Mar 25 22:19:31 2005
From: a.wong at home.nl (Albert Wong)
Date: Fri, 25 Mar 2005 23:19:31 +0100
Subject: Dealing with a dataset that has columns of unequal lengths
Message-ID: <002801c53188$b7704a30$4f01a8c0@AlbertM6N>

As the topic implies, I have a dataset with many 
columns of different lengths. By default, S-Plus expects these columns to be of 
equal length, and will label the 'missing values' as NAs.

 

Now I want to perform MLE parameter estimation 
(GammaMLE, WeibullMLE, LognormalMLE) on each of these columns. But, this will 
work only on the longest column, even when the 'omit missing values' box is 
checked in the 'Asymmetric Parameter Estimation' dialog (of the Robust Library). 
The error message reads 'Problem in (x$coefs[,  , j])[i,  ]: No dim 
attribute for array subset of object of class "logical"'.

 

There's two ways to deal with it, I 
think:

 

1) Override the above mentioned default behaviour 
by S-Plus. According to the User Guide PDF, you're should select the Ragged 
Data.frame check box in the Options -> General Settings -> Data dialog. I 
cannot find this check box anymore. It might have to do with the version; my 
version is S-Plus 6.2, could it be that they removed this option in the newest 
version? If so, is there any way (perhaps by means of regedit.exe) to enforce 
this override?

 

2) Is there a command in S-Plus to omit the NA's, 
in combination with those GammaMLE (and so on) commands?

 

Thanks,

 

Kajun

From myersdb at missouri.edu  Fri Mar 25 22:57:27 2005
From: myersdb at missouri.edu (Brent Myers)
Date: Fri, 25 Mar 2005 16:57:27 -0600
Subject: Tip: Publication Quality Plots
Message-ID: <UM-EXPROTO891rGGXP900003a7c@um-exproto8.um.umsystem.edu>

S-Users,

 

One question that frequently comes up is - how to export
publication quality plots from S code or GUI plots, usually in a windows
environment. I am an S-Plus 2000 holdover, so I am not sure how the image
exports work in 6.X, but they are useless in S-Plus 2000. I have made great use
of the Powerpoint export wizard, but when ungrouping these vector objects to
add a superscript or other text symbol for instance, or to improve the
composition of the plots, they tend to ‘fall apart’. 

 

So, looking for a solution for a particularly reticent
graphic, I employed the Adobe universal pdf print driver – installed with
Adobe Acrobat or available separately as a free download from Adobe. This returned
the most shockingly clear graphic in a pdf file. This was far superior to using
the pdf.graph device as one could use gui plots or code based plots and the
output is exactly as appears in the graphsheet. Output looks very different
with pdf.graph and good results require mastery of that device.

 

This is particularly useful if you have access to the full
version of Adobe Acrobat. With the text touchup tool you can add superscripts
and subscripts. Even better, if you have access to Adobe Illustrator, you
options for text editing and graphics touchup are greatly expanded. The pdf
file can be directly opened and edited natively. Several export formats are
available from there. Scaling in Illustrator is far superior to scaling a wmf
object in Powerpoint.

 

Use of commercial product names is not an endorsement.

 

D. Brenton Myers

Research Specialist

University of Missouri / USDA-ARS-Croping Systems and Water Quality
Unit

 

 

