Hi Jianhe,

you probably will need to do some reading on tree models to be able to use
them correctly and efficiently. I suggest the documentation to the library
rpart, which you can find by googling "rpart". It's a pdf with clear
explanations and good examples. The library rpart is an alternative to the
function tree() that some people (including me) prefer. Brian Ripley
maintains a version of rpart compiled for easy installation on a Windows
based Splus program.
CART stands for classification and regression trees and is a trademark for a
software as far as I know. I don't think that there is a substantial
difference between the functions implemented in CART and the functions in
Splus and the library rpart, but I haven't used CART so I cannot tell you
for sure.
The information you gave is not enough for me to be sure what you mean with
your questions. Is your dependent variable continuous (in which case you
would be doing a regression tree, which I think you are doing), or is it
categorical in which case you would be doing a classification tree.
yval stands for y value, which is the predicted value for your dependent
variable in the given node. In other words, under all the conditions you had
to follow in the independent variables to get to that node, the model
predicts the dependent variable to have this value. I'm not sure what you
mean by "how should I group my data?" What do you want to group and why?

HTH

Volker

