Message-ID: <01a901c535fe$40b1b300$83d1aa87@zhaopc>

Dear S-news subscriber,

 

Does anybody know an easy way to do the 
following without using a loop? 

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Input: x, a vector of string with each element 
consists of comma-delimited fields

output: y, a list with each element of y = each 
element of x separated into several strings.

 

> x
[1] "aaa,bbb"  "cc,dd,ee" 
"ffff" 

     
> 
y
[[1]]:
[1] "aaa" "bbb"

[[2]]:
[1] "cc" "dd" "ee"

[[3]]:
[1] "ffff"

I believe there are many functions in Splus that 
deal with strings manipulation. For example, the "gsub" Prof. Venables mentioned 
recently. I won't have known it if I did not read his email. Is there 
a book/documents telling Splus users what 
functions are available for string manipulation? 

 

Thanks,

Max Zhao

 

 

From dimitrios.rizopoulos at student.kuleuven.ac.be  Thu Mar 31 14:37:38 2005
From: dimitrios.rizopoulos at student.kuleuven.ac.be (Dimitris Rizopoulos)
Date: Thu, 31 Mar 2005 16:37:38 +0200
Subject: Re: A question about String manipulation?
References: <B998A44C8986644EA8029CFE6396A9241B2FA1@exqld2-bne.qld.csiro.au> <01a901c535fe$40b1b300$83d1aa87@zhaopc>
Message-ID: <005401c535ff$2fe9c7f0$0540210a@www.domain>

try this:

x <- c("aaa,bbb" , "cc,dd,ee" , "ffff")
lapply(x, function(y) unlist(unpaste(y, sep=",")))

I hope it helps.

Best,
Dimitris

