>I am drawing a PCA score plot and the key may cover some of the 
>observations no matter which corner I put it. Is there a way that I can 
>put the key outside the axes? 

This works for me:

par(mfrow=c(2,2))
plot(1:3)
key(text = "some text", x = 4)

If you want to place the key using the "corner" argument, 
create a copy of key(), and change the call to approx() near the
top to allow linear extrapolation:
        lambda <- approx(0:1, c(1, 99)/100, corner, rule=3)$y
Then this works:

par(mfrow=c(2,2))
plot(1:3)
key(text = "some text", corner = c(1.5,0)) # fails

>Also, I have a function below that I defined by myself. Whenever I call 
>this function, a string "NULL" is returned. Is there any hint on why this 
>is happening?

Sundar Dorai-Raj <sundar.dorai-raj at pdf.com> answered:
>The value of the last line of your function is automatically returned. 
>In this case, key returns NULL. What did you want your function to return?
To expand on that, key() normally returns invisible() which is
the same as invisible(NULL); so your function returns NULL.  If you
want your function to return NULL invisibly, add a last line
        invisible(NULL)

